  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">An updated catalogue of split-GAL4 driver lines for descending neurons in Drosophila melanogaster (2025) in eLife.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">An updated catalogue of split-GAL4 driver lines for descending neurons in Drosophila melanogaster (2025) in eLife is authored by Jessica L. Zung, Shigehiro Namiki and Geoffrey W Meissner. Topics: Drosophila melanogaster, Melanogaster, Biology, Drosophila (subgenus) and Cell type.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">No citations yet.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2025 eLife article is a gold open-access catalogue of split-GAL4 driver lines for descending neurons in <i>Drosophila melanogaster</i>, a resource-oriented contribution aimed at improving genetic access to an important class of neurons in the fly motor system. The record shows an early-stage impact profile: no citations are recorded yet in OpenAlex, and the available attention signals are limited, but the work is openly available in multiple repository instances and is already indexed as a dataset-citing article.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article addresses a clear enabling problem in fly neuroscience. Descending neurons sit at a key junction between brain and body, and the paper updates a catalogue of split-GAL4 driver lines intended to make those cell types more accessible for experimental study. That makes the work primarily a community resource: its value lies less in a single experimental result than in the infrastructure it provides for targeting defined neuronal populations.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The metadata point to a collaborative, cross-institutional effort spanning the Howard Hughes Medical Institute, West Virginia University, the University of Cambridge, and the MRC Laboratory of Molecular Biology. The journal venue, eLife, and the gold open-access status support broad availability, and OpenAIRE records show the article in three open instances, including a preprint version and the published version. This kind of distribution can help researchers adopt the resource quickly, even when formal citation uptake is still minimal.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the impact record is still thin. OpenAlex reports 0 citations, and scite shows no citing, supporting, or contradicting statements yet. Public discourse data are also unavailable from the archive source used here, so there is no reliable basis to describe wider online discussion. In other words, the record is consistent with a recent article whose scholarly and community uptake may still be developing.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There are, however, a few positive signals of reach that fit a methods-and-resources paper. Crossref data-citation processing succeeded, indicating that the article is connected to dataset-citation infrastructure, and WorldCat holdings suggest some library-level discoverability. Taken together, the evidence supports a careful interpretation: this is an openly accessible, community-facing neuroscience resource with clear potential utility, but the present record shows early dissemination rather than established citation impact.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">For readers in Drosophila neurobiology, the significance is likely to be practical first. By expanding and updating genetic tools for descending neurons, the paper helps lower the barrier to functional studies of sensorimotor control. Its scholarly footprint may grow as other groups use these lines in downstream experiments and begin citing the catalogue as part of that workflow.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
